<p>Benin voted Sunday in a presidential succession race to replace term-limited President Patrice Talon, with Romuald Wadagni widely expected to win. The vote took place under security pressure in the country&#x27;s north. In neighboring Djibouti, President Ismail Omar Guelleh won re-election to a sixth term in a contest that analysts said offered little genuine competition. Together, the two elections show how continuity and stability remain politically dominant themes in parts of Africa, even as questions persist about how competitive these races really are.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/W6Kx0eTDsEaK789Qx3j1x0D0nxzQhM2cGndTJkbY4ZlF0G4WrLnTG1PWCyqlPMkYX_jlTai-v2Z8cdN1_oSvS1iEb5xWoqMHwDFFUM4oX27lS78cKAF6_7sTcK7bRP_upWjgsudmfNEXnysZqs3mHU5ehRbXolywOosMARgo7zfiZ70AlFQEwg3T0DCS2GdASTNq' alt='Djibouti presidential election scene linked to Ismail Omar Guelleh&#x27;s re-election' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Seven-year term: The winner of Benin&#x27;s election will serve as president for the next seven years.</li><li>Limited choice: Only two candidates appeared on Benin&#x27;s presidential ballot.</li><li>Growth factor: Reports said Wadagni entered the race backed by Benin&#x27;s strong economic growth and the absence of a credible challenger.</li><li>Single-opponent race: Guelleh faced one challenger in Djibouti&#x27;s presidential contest.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Reuters: Reuters described Benin&#x27;s vote as a succession election to replace Patrice Talon, with the winner set to govern for the next seven years. (<a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/benin-finance-minister-expected-coast-presidential-election-win-2026-04-12/'>Reuters</a>)</li><li>The Straits Times: The Straits Times highlighted Wadagni&#x27;s edge, pointing to strong economic growth and the absence of a credible challenger. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/benin-finance-minister-expected-to-coast-to-presidential-election-win'>The Straits Times</a>)</li>